  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nBroad aggregate mid and upper-level troughing over the north \ncentral CONUS will steer a few smaller scale impulses across our\narea, resulting in a few clusters of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the region today into tonight. One such wave is currently\ndriving a line of heavy rain producing showers and storms \neastward across northern IL. Fortunately, the eastward movement \n~25 mph is limiting the duration of heavy rainfall at any given \nlocation, thus limiting the flash flood threat, at least in the \nnear term. Nevertheless, a quick localized inch of rain may fall\nunder the strongest cores. \n\nFollowing this initial line of showers and storms, we will be \nmonitoring the trends with the ongoing cluster of storms over \nIA. A good amount of available guidance suggests that this \nactivity will track eastward into northwestern IL around \ndaybreak. Thereafter, it remains a bit unclear as to how long it\nmay hold together as it moves into northeastern IL (especially \nnorth of I-80) during the mid-morning hours, owing to the poor \ndiurnal timing and modest instability. Nevertheless, the current\nthinking is that there will at least be a region of weakening \nshowers moving into the Chicago metro area (mainly in the 8-11 \nam timeframe). Should this activity hold together into \nnortheastern IL this morning, the presence of deep rich moisture\n(precipitable water values nearly to 2\") will support locally \nheavy downpours.\n\nAfter a break in the shower activity this afternoon, another \nround of developing convection is probable near the surface \nfrontal boundary late this afternoon and evening in response to \nthe approach of the next mid-level impulse. However, with \ndiurnal destabilization expected to remain limited across far \nnorthern IL (especially north of I-88 where temperatures will\nstay in the 70s) as a result of persistent cloud cover through \nthe afternoon, the main focus for stronger convection will \nlikely end up south of I-80. This thinking continues to match \nthe SPC's current Day 1 outlook, which indicates a level 1 of 5 \nsevere threat into west central IL. The main threat with any \nstronger storms would be locally strong damaging wind gusts and \nlocally heavy rainfall, particularly associated with any strong \nstorm clusters. The overall threat of flash flooding will \nultimately be dependent upon if and where multiple storms to \nmove over the same areas (training). While this possibility \ncannot be ruled out on a localized basis in parts of our area, \nthe better potential for this may end up remaining south- \nsouthwest of our area.\n\nThe focus for active weather should largely shift south of the \narea for Thursday and Friday. However, active weather (with \nshower and thunderstorm chances) is likely to return eighter \nthis weekend, or early next week.\n\nKJB\n\n\nWeekend onward:\n\nAs aggregate upper-level troughing shifts eastward toward the \nend of the week, a quasi-zonal to northwesterly upper-level flow\npattern will materialize across the Upper Great Lakes region \nsupporting additional waves of showers and storms. Some of these\nmay be rambunctious as the eastern edge of an EML plume is \nrepeatedly re-advected eastward atop a stalled frontal boundary\ndraped across the Great Lakes. Naturally, confidence in \nwhen/where eventual thunderstorm clusters will roam is low at \nthis range. With that said, the pattern matches conceptual \nmodels for threats for severe weather and flash flooding in the \ngeneral region this weekend into early next week. \n\nBorchardt/KJB",
